														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4144614">
									

<p>This is neat. What's interesting here is that the article doesn't really pass judgment on anything - it doesn't decree that the things that are causing people distress are good or bad, just that they might be causing them distress. This isn't unreasonable, I think - even people who make difficult decisions for their own good or the good of others might feel lingering guilt or uncertainty about them.</p>
<p>The bit about reconciliation at the end is a little weak, but I don't think it's out of place. People who are suffering often reach out to those closest to them for support. Having to face the problems that cause them grief would obviously trigger a similar response, to the point where I'm not even sure that part is anomalous.</p>
<p>Pretty good. For an article as thin as this one is, there's a lot to think about here.</p>

							</div>

								
					
